{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791125437,"nonce":"rUHyiQFHLR61RFzCrhkeB/p27/o=","title":"LLVM IR stays live after codegen: about 34MB of llvm::User in a 50s ashui run","message":"MallocStackLogging on ashui's components gallery (tools/snapshot/run.sh --window tools/snapshot/scenes/ComponentsGallery.hx), 50s in, shows compiler state still live after its compiles finished:\n- 257,860 blocks, 33.7MB: `new(unsigned long, llvm::User::IntrusiveOperandsAllocMarker)` (LLVM IR operands)\n- 58,383 blocks, 11.8MB: air::v2 `Function::clone`\n- 17,736 blocks, 14.3MB: Vec\u003cair::opcodes::Opcode\u003e\n- 786 blocks, 9.2MB: llvm::StringMapImpl::RehashTable\n\nThat is roughly 70-100MB held after the code is installed, out of a footprint of about 690MB (ashui's own live heap is about 14MB).\n\nKnown so far (ash-fd): modules and contexts are leaked deliberately, because old code may still be on a stack, and MCJIT keeps each module's IR beside its code. Freeing the IR, and the AIR clones, once code is installed would be a separate change from keeping the code itself alive.\n\nTo reproduce: run with MallocStackLogging=lite, then `malloc_history \u003cpid\u003e -callTree -invert -collapseRecursion -ignoreThreads` and total by caller.\n\nReported from ashui.","files":null}]}